# Multi-plane spike: 2 Coders + middle + portal + Keycloak

Validates **plane branching**: mint and open apps on the correct control plane.
Alice has workspaces on **A and B**; Bob only on **B**.

## Architecture

```text
Keycloak (:8080)
    │  IdP JWT
Portal (:5173) ──► Middle (:8081) ──► Coder A (:3000)   alice-a
                      │            └► Coder B (:3001)   alice-b, bob-b
                      │
                      └─ GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/coder-app/{slug}?plane=a|b
                           → Set-Cookie coder_session_token (host-only) + 302 to
                             http://127.0.0.1:PORT/@user/ws/apps/slug/  (no token in URL)
```

Apps are **path-based** (`subdomain = false`): **ttyd** (terminal) + **code-server**.
Built-in Coder web terminal is disabled (`display_apps.web_terminal = false`).

## Quick start

```bash
# 1. Keycloak
docker compose -f local-sso/docker-compose.yml up -d

# 2. Two Coder planes (separate terminals)
PLANE_ID=a PORT=3000 ./spike-middle/multi-plane-lab/start-plane.sh
PLANE_ID=b PORT=3001 ./spike-middle/multi-plane-lab/start-plane.sh

# 3. Seed users + template + workspaces
chmod +x spike-middle/multi-plane-lab/*.sh
./spike-middle/multi-plane-lab/seed-planes.sh

# 4. Fill owner tokens into config
cp spike-middle/multi-plane-lab/config.multiplane.yaml /tmp/mp-config.yaml
# paste tokens from /tmp/multiplane-lab/owner-a.token and owner-b.token into planes.*.owner_token

# 5. Middle
cd spike-middle/go-middle
CONFIG_FILE=/tmp/multiplane-lab/config.yaml go run .
# or: go build -o /tmp/multiplane-middle . && CONFIG_FILE=… /tmp/multiplane-middle

# 6. Portal
cd spike-middle/portal && MIDDLE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081 npm start
```

Open http://127.0.0.1:5173 → Keycloak as **alice** / **alice** or **bob** / **bob**.

| User | Expected workspaces |
| --- | --- |
| alice | `alice-a` (plane **a**), `alice-b` (plane **b**) |
| bob | `bob-b` (plane **b**) only |

Coder UI (optional): email `alice@example.com` / `bob@example.com`, password `TestUser1!`.

## Host-only cookie (no token in the URL)

### Goal

Open the **real** Coder app URL without putting `?coder_session_token=` in the address bar
(history / Referer / logs).

### How it works (local lab)

1. Portal calls `POST /v1/app-session` (sets `middle_session`), then navigates to  
   `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/coder-app/{slug}?plane=…`.
2. Middle mints/reuses the user’s Coder API token for that plane.
3. Middle responds with:
   - `Set-Cookie: coder_session_token=…; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax`  
     (**no `Domain=` attribute** → **host-only**)
   - `302` → `http://127.0.0.1:PORT/@user/ws/apps/slug/` (clean URL; code-server may keep `?folder=`)
4. Browser sends that cookie to Coder on the same hostname. Coder accepts  
   `Cookie: coder_session_token=…` for path apps (same as query param).

### Why localhost works

Browsers treat cookies as **host-only and ignore port**. Middle (`127.0.0.1:8081`) and
planes (`127.0.0.1:3000` / `:3001`) share hostname `127.0.0.1`, so a cookie set by middle
is sent to both planes.

That is convenient for the lab and awkward for multi-plane: opening plane B **overwrites**
the shared `coder_session_token` for the host. Production should use **distinct hostnames**
per plane (`coder-a.example.com`, `coder-b.example.com`) and set the host-only cookie from
**that plane’s edge** (or a bootstrap path on that host)—not `Domain=.example.com`
(which would leak one session to every subdomain).

### TTLs / logout

| Cookie | Max-Age | Cleared by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `coder_session_token` | **2 hours** | `POST /v1/logout` |
| `middle_session` | **12 hours** | `POST /v1/logout` |

Portal Logout → `POST` middle `/v1/logout` (clears both) → Keycloak logout.  
Already-open app tabs may keep working until refresh; a new open after logout should fail auth.

### Auth priority (bob must not see alice)

Portal sends `credentials: "include"` **and** `Authorization: Bearer <IdP JWT>`.  
Middle prefers **Bearer over `middle_session`**, so switching Keycloak users is not stuck
on a stale cookie.

